  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Earth reaches perihelion, its closest point to the Sun, in early January (around January 2 to 5) and aphelion, its farthest, in early July.",
      "source": "NASA Science, 'What Causes the Seasons?' (spaceplace.nasa.gov/seasons); US Naval Observatory, Earth's Seasons and Apsides table (aa.usno.navy.mil/data/EarthSeasons)",
      "checkedBy": null,
      "beats": [
        "l2",
        "l3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Earth's orbit is nearly circular (eccentricity about 0.017); its distance from the Sun changes by only about 3% over the year.",
      "source": "NASA Goddard, Earth Fact Sheet (nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/earthfact.html): perihelion 147.1 million km, aphelion 152.1 million km, eccentricity 0.0167",
      "checkedBy": null,
      "beats": [
        "l4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Seasons are caused by the tilt of Earth's axis (about 23.4°): the hemisphere tilted toward the Sun gets more direct sunlight and longer days, so it has summer.",
      "source": "NASA Science, 'What Causes the Seasons?' (spaceplace.nasa.gov/seasons); OpenStax, Astronomy 2e, §4.2 The Seasons",
      "checkedBy": null,
      "beats": [
        "l6",
        "l8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The Southern Hemisphere (e.g. Australia) has summer in December to February, when the Northern Hemisphere has winter.",
      "source": "OpenStax, Astronomy 2e, §4.2 The Seasons",
      "checkedBy": null,
      "beats": [
        "l7"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "simplifications": [
    "The 'myth' orbit on screen is drawn far too oval on purpose; the real orbit (the second state) is nearly a circle.",
    "The two globes are lit from screen angles to show which hemisphere leans toward the Sun; the Sun, sizes and distances are not to scale.",
    "Distance has a small effect on sunlight (about 7% more in January than July), but the tilt wins by far."
  ],
